# Lovable sync workflow

This repo is two-way synced with a Lovable project. Lovable only reads and writes `main`, and every Lovable edit lands on `main` as a commit. Anything merged to `main` on GitHub shows up in Lovable.

## Every change from Cursor

1. `git checkout main && git pull` (Lovable commits constantly, so always start fresh).
2. Create a branch, make the change, commit.
3. `git push -u origin <branch>`, then open a PR with `gh pr create`.
4. Merge on GitHub with squash (`gh pr merge --squash`).
5. `git checkout main && git pull` again.

## Never

- Push directly to `main`. Don't even commit on `main`: `npm install` runs a `postinstall` that sets `core.hooksPath` to `.githooks`, and `.githooks/post-commit` then pushes every commit immediately.
- Force-push or rebase `main`.
- Rename or move the GitHub repo (it breaks the Lovable link).
- Edit files in Lovable while an open PR touches them.
- Commit secrets. Shopify tokens and `SHOPIFY_MULTIPASS_SECRET` live in Lovable/Supabase settings. `.env` only holds public keys (`VITE_SUPABASE_*`).
- Rewrite `mem/`. It is Lovable's memory: read it for context, don't edit it from Cursor.
- Use em dashes or en dashes in any text (see `mem/preferences/no-em-dashes.md`).
- Commit the test account password.

## Testing

- `npm run dev` runs the screens locally, but they call the live Supabase edge functions. A function change only takes effect after it is merged and deployed, so local runs exercise the old function code until then.
- Sign-in test account: `dd-test-trial@example.com`. Ask the user for the password each session.

## Deploys

`mem/index.md` says "Frontend manual, Backend auto": the frontend goes live only when someone clicks Publish in Lovable, while backend changes deploy on their own. That describes Lovable-made edits. For changes merged from GitHub:

1. **Edge functions do NOT redeploy on a GitHub merge (confirmed 2026-10-05, PR #5).** Production still ran the old code more than 6 minutes after the merge. After merging a change to `supabase/functions/<name>/`, ask Lovable's agent (Lovable MCP `send_message`, or the user in the editor) to deploy that function with no code changes, naming only the changed functions.
2. **Migrations merged from GitHub: still UNVERIFIED.** Confirm on the first PR that adds one, then update this item. Until then, assume Lovable must apply it and check that the schema change actually exists.

Measured on 2026-10-05 (login PR A rollback rehearsal, PRs #8 to #10): merge to new code live took 1 to 1.5 minutes each way when Lovable's agent was asked right after the merge (`send_message` with `wait: false`, then poll the header every 15 s). A function rollback is a revert PR plus that deploy request.

Verify a function deploy instead of trusting the deploy message. Have the function return a version header (for example `X-Function-Version`) and check it with `curl.exe -s -D - -o NUL` against the live URL. An unauthenticated request is enough, since the 401 response carries the header too.

Lovable's agent may fix unrelated build errors while it is there and commit them to `main`. Review those commits (`git log`, `git show --stat`) after each request.
